(c) stay uphill at all times, if possible;
(d) seal off an extensive area surrounding the scene of
the crash or disaster;
(e) locate the exit point from the aircraft and if the
cabin crew is conscious, leave evacuation to them, if
not, assist in evacuation;
(f) keep onlookers as far as possible from the aircraft
and in an uphill, upwind position;
(g) prohibit smoking by survivors, rescuers and
onlookers;
(h) prohibit the removal of any part of the crash
aircraft, except by investigators;
(i) where the crash occurs in a residential area, advise
the residents to do the following:
(i) shut all doors of their premises and remove
any curtain material that could spread fire
in the event of an explosion;
(ii) turn off gas and electricity at the mains
and switch off all machinery;
(iii) extinguish any existing fire, whether
internal or external;
(iv) refrain from smoking or striking matches;
(v) evacuate the building, if necessary, by
doors and windows on the opposite side of
the building from the crash;
(j) notwithstanding the guidelines mentioned, Police
Officers are required to observe the principles
stated in the Schedule hereto.
